---
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import { OPENAPI, UNDOCUMENTED_MOUNTS, OPERATIONS } from '../../data/openapi.js';

const successEnvelope = `{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Success",
  "data": { "_id": "665f1c2a9b4e7d0012a3b4c5", "name": "Acme Corp" }
}`;

const paginatedEnvelope = `{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Success",
  "data": [ /* records */ ],
  "pagination": {
    "total": 137,
    "page": 1,
    "limit": 50,
    "totalPages": 3,
    "hasNext": true,
    "hasPrev": false
  }
}`;

const errorEnvelope = `{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Validation failed: Client Number cannot be blank",
  "errors": ["Client Number cannot be blank"],
  "fieldErrors": { "client_number": "Client Number cannot be blank" }
}`;

const STATUSES = [
  ['200', 'OK', 'Request succeeded.'],
  ['201', 'Created', 'A resource was created. Returned by POST endpoints.'],
  ['400', 'Bad Request', 'Field validation failed, a validation rule was triggered, or the payload was malformed.'],
  ['401', 'Unauthorized', 'No valid API key or session. Also returned when a key is revoked or expired.'],
  ['403', 'Forbidden', 'Authenticated, but the user lacks the permission — or the API key lacks the scope — for this action.'],
  ['404', 'Not Found', 'The object or record does not exist within your tenant. Note that a record in another tenant is a 404, never a 403.'],
  ['409', 'Conflict', 'A uniqueness constraint was violated, e.g. creating a field whose apiName is taken.'],
  ['429', 'Too Many Requests', 'A rate-limited route was called too often. See Rate Limits.'],
  ['500', 'Server Error', 'Unexpected failure. The response body omits internals outside development.'],
];
---

<MainLayout
  title="API Overview"
  description="Base URL, authentication, response envelope, status codes and conventions for the Salesdam REST API."
>
  <h1>API Overview</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Salesdam exposes a REST API over the same metadata that drives the app. One
    set of record endpoints serves every object — standard and custom — addressed
    by its <code>apiName</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Base URL</h2>
  <p>
    Every route is mounted under the <code>/api/v1</code> prefix. In development
    the API listens on port 5000:
  </p>
  <pre><code>http://localhost:5000/api/v1</code></pre>
  <p>
    In production, use the same origin as the app. There is exactly one API
    version today; <code>v1</code> is part of the path rather than a header, so a
    future version can run alongside it.
  </p>

  <h2>Authentication</h2>
  <p>
    Two mechanisms, checked in this order. There is <strong>no bearer-token
    scheme</strong> — an <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code> header is ignored and
    the request is treated as unauthenticated.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Header / cookie</th><th>Use for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>API key</strong></td>
          <td><code>X-API-Key: sk_live_…</code></td>
          <td>Server-to-server integrations, scripts, webhooks.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Session cookie</strong></td>
          <td><code>sid</code>, set by <code>POST /auth/login</code></td>
          <td>The web app itself, and same-origin browser requests.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    See <a href="/api/authentication">Authentication</a> for scopes, IP
    allow-lists and tenant selection.
  </p>

  <h2>Response envelope</h2>
  <p>
    Every successful response is wrapped in the same shape, so a client can check
    one field to know whether to read <code>data</code> or <code>message</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{successEnvelope}</code></pre>

  <p>List endpoints add a <code>pagination</code> block alongside <code>data</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>{paginatedEnvelope}</code></pre>

  <p>Failures keep <code>success: false</code> and describe what went wrong:</p>
  <pre><code>{errorEnvelope}</code></pre>

  <div class="note">
    <strong><code>errors</code> and <code>fieldErrors</code> appear on validation
    failures only.</strong> <code>errors</code> is a flat list suitable for a
    summary; <code>fieldErrors</code> is keyed by field API name for highlighting
    individual inputs. See <a href="/api/error-handling">Errors</a>.
  </div>

  <h2>Status codes</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>When</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {STATUSES.map(([code, name, when]) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{code}</code></td>
            <td>{name}</td>
            <td>{when}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Conventions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Field values are top-level.</strong> A record's custom fields are
      sent and returned as top-level keys, not nested under a <code>data</code>
      property — the storage shape is not the wire shape.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Computed fields are read-only.</strong> Formula, rollup summary and
      auto-number values are produced server-side. Sending them is ignored.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tenancy is implicit.</strong> Every query is scoped to the caller's
      tenant. A record belonging to another tenant returns <code>404</code>, not
      <code>403</code>, so the API never confirms that an id exists elsewhere.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>PATCH is partial, PUT is not.</strong> Both run the object's
      validation rules against the merged result.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dates are ISO 8601 in UTC.</strong> Date-only fields are stored at
      UTC midnight.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Coverage</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/api/reference">Endpoint Explorer</a> documents
    <strong>{OPERATIONS.length} endpoints</strong> across {OPENAPI.tags.length}
    resource groups — the surface an integration normally needs.
  </p>
  <p>
    These routes are mounted but not yet described. They work; their request and
    response shapes are simply not documented, so treat them as unstable:
  </p>
  <p class="mounts">
    {UNDOCUMENTED_MOUNTS.map((m) => <code>{m}</code>)}
  </p>
</MainLayout>

<style>
  .mounts {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--spacing-2);
  }
</style>
